      "text": "To determine the first day on which the colony of bacteria has more than 100 bacteria, we start by understanding the growth pattern of the bacteria. The colony starts with 3 bacteria and doubles every day. This can be expressed by the formula:\n\n\\[ N = 3 \\times 2^{d} \\]\n\nwhere \\( N \\) is the number of bacteria at the end of day \\( d \\).\n\nWe need to find the smallest \\( d \\) such that \\( N > 100 \\). Therefore, we set up the inequality:\n\n\\[ 3 \\times 2^d > 100 \\]\n\nFirst, we isolate \\( 2^d \\) by dividing both sides of the inequality by 3:\n\n\\[ 2^d > \\frac{100}{3} \\]\n\\[ 2^d > 33.33 \\]\n\nNext, we need to find the smallest integer \\( d \\) such that \\( 2^d \\) is greater than 33.33. We can do this by evaluating successive powers of 2:\n\n\\[ 2^5 = 32 \\]\n\\[ 2^6 = 64 \\]\n\nSince \\( 2^5 = 32 \\) is less than 33.33 and \\( 2^6 = 64 \\) is greater than 33.33, the smallest \\( d \\) that satisfies the inequality is 6.\n\nTo confirm, we check the number of bacteria at the end of day 6:\n\n\\[ N = 3 \\times 2^6 = 3 \\times 64 = 192 \\]\n\nSince 192 is greater than 100, the first day on which the colony has more than 100 bacteria is day 6.\n\nThus, the answer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{6}\n\\]",
